# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
berkeley.md:1: argue/reported-as-premise: C1 (S14): C1 rests on P8, which reports what locke says: a work's account of another's claim is not its premise; if the work holds it, it says so on a line of its own
berkeley.md:1: argue/readings: S12 is read 2 ways: berkeley.logic has nothing from it; berkeley.logic.2 and berkeley.logic.3 have "distinguishing → many_men_brutes"; they differ when only "abstraction is made the distinguishing property of brutes as against men" is true, which the first allows
berkeley.md:1: argue/answered: S12: answered no, which keeps berkeley.logic.2 and berkeley.logic.3, to: Suppose "abstraction is made the distinguishing property of brutes as against men" is true and "a great many of those that pass for men must be reckoned into the number of brutes" is false. Is S12, "But then if this be made the distinguishing property of that sort of animals, I fear a great many of those that pass for men must be reckoned into their number.", true then?
berkeley.md:1: argue/readings: S13 is read 2 ways: berkeley.logic and berkeley.logic.2 have nothing from it; berkeley.logic.3 has "words_imply_general"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
berkeley.md:1: argue/answered: S13: answered yes, which keeps berkeley.logic and berkeley.logic.2, to: Suppose "the making use of words implies the having general ideas" is false. Is S13, "The reason that is here assigned why we have no grounds to think brutes have abstract general ideas is, that we observe in them no use of words or any other general signs; which is built on this supposition--that the making use of words implies the having general ideas.", true then?
berkeley.md:1: argue/readings: S14 is read 2 ways: berkeley.logic and berkeley.logic.2 have "words_imply_general → men_abstract_lang"; berkeley.logic.3 has nothing from it; they differ when only "the making use of words implies the having general ideas" is true, which the second allows
berkeley.md:1: argue/answered: S14: answered no, which keeps berkeley.logic and berkeley.logic.2, to: Suppose "the making use of words implies the having general ideas" is true and "men who use language are able to abstract or generalize their ideas" is false. Is S14, "From which it follows that men who use language are able to ABSTRACT or GENERALIZE their ideas.", true then?
berkeley.logic: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: berkeley.logic and berkeley.logic.2 have nothing from it; berkeley.logic.3 has "words_imply_general → men_abstract_lang"; they differ when only "the making use of words implies the having general ideas" is true, which the first allows
berkeley.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: berkeley.logic and berkeley.logic.2 have "sign_particulars ∧ ¬sign_abstract"; berkeley.logic.3 has "men_abstract_lang"; they differ when only "a word becomes general by being made the sign of several particular ideas, any one of which it indifferently suggests to the mind" is true, which the first allows
berkeley.logic: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: berkeley.logic and berkeley.logic.2 put it at S18, "But it seems that a word becomes general by being made the sign, not of an ABSTRACT general idea, but of several particular ideas, any one of which it indifferently suggests to the mind."; berkeley.logic.3 at S14, "From which it follows that men who use language are able to ABSTRACT or GENERALIZE their ideas."
berkeley.logic: argue/answered: M: answered yes, which keeps berkeley.logic and berkeley.logic.2, to: Is the text's main conclusion S18, "But it seems that a word becomes general by being made the sign, not of an ABSTRACT general idea, but of several particular ideas, any one of which it indifferently suggests to the mind.", rather than S14, "From which it follows that men who use language are able to ABSTRACT or GENERALIZE their ideas."?
berkeley.md:1: argue/negation: berkeley.logic.3 P8 (S13): the sentence says "no", but P8 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "the making use of words implies the having general ideas"
berkeley.logic: argue/verdicts: valid as berkeley.logic.3 reads it, not as the others do: which reading is right decides the verdict
