# argument written to berkeley.logic by claude-opus-5-5 (a language model, not a rule)
berkeley.md:1: argue/reported-as-premise: C1 (S14): C1 rests on P8, which reports what locke says: a work's account of another's claim is not its premise; if the work holds it, it says so on a line of its own
berkeley.md:1: argue/unlinked: C (S18): the conclusion speaks of "a word becomes general by being made the sign of an abstract general idea", which no premise mentions: nothing links it to them
berkeley.md:1: argue/unlinked: C (S18): the conclusion speaks of "a word becomes general by being made the sign of several particular ideas, any one of which it indifferently suggests to the mind", which no premise mentions: nothing links it to them
berkeley.md:1: argue/invalid: C1 (S14): does not follow: when nothing is true, every premise holds and the conclusion fails
berkeley.md:1: argue/countermodel: C1 (S14): men_abstract_lang = F, words_imply_general = F
berkeley.md:1: argue/missing: C1 (S14): it would follow given "words_imply_general" (the making use of words implies the having general ideas), or "¬words_imply_general → men_abstract_lang"
berkeley.md:1: argue/unlinked: P9 (S14): P9 is the only statement about "the making use of words implies the having general ideas": as written it cannot bear on the conclusion; was the same thing named two ways?
berkeley.md:1: argue/invalid: C (S18): does not follow: when "men who use language are able to abstract or generalize their ideas" is true, "a word becomes general by being made the sign of an abstract general idea" and "a word becomes general by being made the sign of several particular ideas, any one of which it indifferently suggests to the mind" are false, and nothing else is true, every premise holds and the conclusion fails
berkeley.md:1: argue/countermodel: C (S18): abstract_brutes = F, distinguishing = F, many_men_brutes = F, men_abstract_lang = T, sign_abstract = F, sign_particulars = F
berkeley.md:1: argue/unlinked: P5 (S11): P5 is the only statement about "brutes have the power of abstracting, or making general ideas": as written it cannot bear on the conclusion; was the same thing named two ways?
berkeley.md:1: argue/unlinked: P6 (S12): P6 is the only statement about "abstraction is made the distinguishing property of brutes as against men" and "a great many of those that pass for men must be reckoned into the number of brutes": as written it cannot bear on the conclusion; was the same thing named two ways?
berkeley.md:1: argue/unlinked: C1 (S14): C1 is the only statement about "men who use language are able to abstract or generalize their ideas": as written it cannot bear on the conclusion; was the same thing named two ways?
